Question 1
A cross-cutting concern is functionality used by many classes, such as logging, security, or transactions.
Tangling means business logic and logging/security code are mixed together in OrderService.
Scattering means the same logging/security code is repeated in many classes.

Question 2
  - Aspect: class containing cross-cutting logic.
  - Join point: point where advice can run.
  - Pointcut: rule that selects join points.
  - Advice: code that runs before/after a method.
  - Target: original bean being advised.
  - Proxy: Spring-created wrapper around the target.
In Spring AOP, a join point is always a method execution.

Question 3
  - @Before: runs before the target method.
  - @AfterReturning: runs after normal return.
  - @AfterThrowing: runs when an exception is thrown.
  - @After: runs finally, whether success or failure.
  - @Around: runs before and after; controls target execution.
Only @Around can replace the return value or prevent the target from running by not calling proceed().

Question 4
Common @Around mistakes:
  - Forgetting proceed() → target method never runs.
  - Calling proceed() twice → target method runs twice.
  - Returning the wrong value or null → caller receives an incorrect result.

Question 5
  execution(* com.example.orders.service.*.*(..))
Matches any method in any class directly inside service.
  
  execution(* com.example.orders..*(..))
Matches any method in com.example.orders and all subpackages.

  execution(* *(Long,..))
Matches any method whose first parameter is Long.

  @annotation(com.example.orders.app.LogExecutionTime)
Matches methods annotated with @LogExecutionTime.

Question 6
Spring AOP creates a proxy that wraps the target bean. Calls through the proxy run advice before/after the target method.
The proxy is usually created during BeanPostProcessor.postProcessAfterInitialization().
Check whether a bean is a proxy with:
  AopUtils.isAopProxy(bean)

Question 7
Calling an annotated method from another method in the same class is called self-invocation. It bypasses the Spring proxy, so advice does not run.
Fixes:
  - Move the annotated method into another Spring bean.
  - Inject the bean’s own proxy and call through that proxy.

Question 8
By default, @Transactional rolls back for unchecked exceptions (RuntimeException and Error), but not checked exceptions.
No rollback may happen when:
  - A checked exception is thrown.
  - The exception is caught and not rethrown.
  - noRollbackFor is configured for that exception.

Question 9
SecurityAspect has order 1, so it is outermost:
  Security before
  Timing before
  Target method
  Timing after
  Security after
Spring AOP uses runtime proxies and supports method-execution join points.
AspectJ uses compile-time or load-time weaving and supports more join points, including constructors, field access, and method execution.
